$cats['Sketch']['Sketch Push Sketch'] = @('A human skull appears to have consumed an entire drawing session. Dozens of failed skulls surround and pass through one another. Different jaw positions eye sockets cranial shapes and perspective attempts accumulate into a dense cloud until one final skull emerges in brutally confident black. The previous attempts must remain readable enough that the viewer can reconstruct how the final form was discovered','A beautiful womans portrait begins delicately near the face but becomes increasingly unstable outward. Hair turns into black charcoal gestures. One shoulder becomes anatomical construction. Red marks search for an alternate head position. A second faint face appears beneath the final one. The entire tattoo should feel like the artist kept drawing until one woman finally emerged from every possible version of her','A colossal skeletal dragon uses the human back as its anatomy. The dragons vertebral column follows the wearers spine. Its rib cage opens around the mid back. The skull turns over one shoulder. Huge wing studies spread across both shoulder blades with several earlier wing positions remaining visible in red and black construction. Parts of the creature are complete bone anatomy while others remain frantic unfinished studies','Two lovers face one another across the womans upper back. One person is fully resolved through elegant black drawing. The second is almost absent existing only through searching lines repeated eyes faint hands and unfinished contours. Where their foreheads meet both drawings become equally dark for one small area','A medieval knight falls backward at the instant of defeat. Three ghosted positions of the body show the trajectory of the fall. The sword exists in several positions. Armor construction lines remain visible. Behind him an enormous dragon is barely resolved at all appearing primarily through eyes teeth horns and violent charcoal marks','A fallen angel occupies the womans upper back. The human figure is beautifully and delicately drawn but each wing explores a different stage of creation. One wing reveals skeletal bird anatomy one section adds muscle and feather construction and another becomes fully feathered before disintegrating into black charcoal across the shoulder','A Gothic cathedral appears to be constructed around a human skeleton. The skeleton occupies the center while ribs gradually become architectural arches vertebrae become the central tower and perspective lines extend outward into unfinished flying buttresses. Red corrections and black final lines reveal several competing versions of the impossible building','A dragon begins at the wrist as a loose blue anatomical construction. Traveling upward red corrections introduce muscles scales and altered proportions. By the upper arm the creature becomes an intensely confident black dragon but its enormous wings break apart again into raw charcoal as they cross the shoulder. The entire arm shows one creature moving from first idea to finished drawing and then exploding beyond completion','A womans face occupies one shoulder blade but it is constructed from dozens of earlier faces. Eyes appear in slightly different positions. Lips repeat. Jawlines overlap. Profiles emerge and disappear. Some attempts are pale blue some burgundy and some black. Only one final pair of eyes reaches full dark resolution. From a distance it is one haunting portrait. Up close it becomes an entire history of trying to draw her','Create the ultimate contemporary Sketch tattoo as a massive dark-fantasy drawing unfolding across the mans chest shoulder and arm. A skeletal dragon emerges from a shattered human skull on the chest. The dragon begins as anatomical construction around the skull becomes a living creature as it crosses the shoulder and then breaks into multiple gestural flight positions down the arm. Its wing anatomy is studied repeatedly in blue construction lines. Red corrections alter joints claws and horn positions. Confident black drawing establishes the final creature. Dense charcoal erupts around selected areas but never becomes meaningless noise. A ruined Gothic cathedral appears faintly behind the skull built from perspective lines that continue through the composition and disappear around the shoulder. The final tattoo must not look like separate images assembled into a collage. It should feel like one extraordinary drawing session in which anatomy architecture mythology movement and darkness gradually discover one another across the human body')
$b['A huge raven is caught at the instant its wings open. The head and chest resolve through confident black drawing while the wings contain repeated searching contours abandoned feather positions and long gestural marks that overshoot the final anatomy'] = 'The tattoo: A huge raven is caught at the instant its wings open. The head and chest resolve through confident black drawing while the wings contain repeated searching contours, abandoned feather positions and long gestural marks that overshoot the final anatomy. Wild, intelligent, raw. Create exceptional contemporary Sketch tattoo artwork where the visible drawing process is the defining visual language. THE DRAWING PROCESS IS THE STYLE. THE DRAWING LOOKS UNFINISHED. THE ARTWORK IS COMPLETELY INTENTIONAL. Do not create a polished tattoo and surround it with random scratches. Do not create ordinary Fine Line artwork with meaningless loose marks. Every unfinished mark must have a visual reason to exist. Use searching contours, repeated attempts, gesture lines, anatomical construction, centerlines, perspective guides, circles, ellipses, proportion marks, abandoned positions, overshooting strokes, hatching, cross-hatching, scribble shading, charcoal-like darkness, broken contours and selective areas of confident finished drawing. SOME LINES SEARCH. SOME LINES MEASURE. SOME LINES MOVE. SOME LINES COMMIT. Allow the viewer to understand how the artist discovered the final image. Use hierarchy. Not every mark should have equal strength. Preliminary construction can be lighter. Corrections can become more assertive. Final committed areas can use darker confident black. Allow some areas to remain almost unresolved while focal areas become sharply understandable. Movement is a major opportunity. Use repeated positions, ghosted limbs, abandoned wing locations, gestural arcs and overlapping anatomy to show where a body, animal or object has just been and where it is moving. Do not use generic cartoon speed lines. Sketch is a visual language, not a subject category. The subject can be anything. Portraits. Human figures. Hands. Animals. Birds. Horses. Dragons. Skulls. Skeletons. Demons. Angels. Dark fantasy. Gothic architecture. Mythological creatures. Horror. Botanicals. Machines. Motorcycles. Cars. Fashion. Architecture. Anatomy. Surrealism. Impossible creatures. Abstract drawing. Do not keep the imagery polite. Some tattoos should be beautiful. Some should be intimate. Some should be elegant. Some should be dark. Some should be aggressive. Some should be disturbing. Some should feel enormous and mythological. Some should make the viewer wonder what the hell they are looking at before the subject suddenly resolves. Push artistic ambition rather than novelty. Do not turn dark artwork into cheap gore or juvenile shock imagery. Darkness should come through composition, anatomy, mythology, mortality, psychological tension, scale, atmosphere and drawing energy. Black and natural skin should dominate much of the collection. Color may be used as part of the drawing process. Pale blue can represent preliminary construction. Red or burgundy can represent correction, alternative positioning or an additional drawing layer. Black can establish the final committed drawing. These relationships are possibilities rather than mandatory rules. Color must not become random decoration. Do not turn Sketch into Watercolour. Do not use full realistic color unless the concept specifically requires a hybrid. Hatching and cross-hatching should describe form and directional shadow. Scribble and charcoal-like marks should carry weight, movement or atmosphere rather than merely making the tattoo look dirty. Construction lines should relate logically to the subject. Human heads may use cranial masses, centerlines and eye axes. Hands may use joint circles and finger direction. Animals may expose skeletal or muscular construction. Machines and architecture may retain perspective axes and ellipses. Dragons and invented creatures should be constructed convincingly enough that their imaginary anatomy feels studied. Design every composition specifically for the stated body placement. THE BODY IS THE PAGE, BUT THE PAGE IS NOT FLAT. Use forearms for elongated drawings, perspective, smaller portraits and technical studies. Use upper arms and shoulders for portraits, creatures and compositions that benefit from wrapping construction. Use thighs for large figures, dark fantasy, horses, dragons and complicated drawing studies. Use calves for strong vertical compositions. Use ribs for gesture and elongated human figures. Use backs for rare major pieces where wings, anatomy, architecture or large drawing systems need space. Let construction and movement respond to body curvature rather than appearing printed onto the skin. Do not make every design huge. Most should remain wearable small and medium pieces. Use large scale where the artistic idea genuinely benefits from it. Reserve full backs, chest compositions and major limb projects for exceptional concepts. Avoid repetitive wolves, lions, roses and womens faces unless the treatment is genuinely exceptional. Avoid random sketch marks. Avoid fake handwritten notes. Avoid meaningless arrows. Avoid decorative geometry unrelated to construction. Avoid generic splatter. Avoid clean finished tattoos with superficial scribbles around them. Avoid making every drawing equally chaotic. Contrast beautifully resolved areas against unresolved areas. THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A BAD SKETCH AND A GREAT SKETCH TATTOO IS INTENTION. A BAD SKETCH LOOKS UNFINISHED BECAUSE IT WAS NOT FINISHED. A GREAT SKETCH TATTOO LOOKS UNFINISHED BECAUSE THAT IS THE FINISHED ART. In the right panel it is on a man, outer upper arm and shoulder, allowing the wing movement to spread around the deltoid'
